I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern format, and the supplied refinement utilities first. Then I’ll screen phases, audit candidate structures, build and compare full-pattern GSAS-II models, and finish by replay-auditing every exported deliverable.

The chemistry points first toward manganese tungstate formed from MnO and WO₃, but I’m treating that only as a hypothesis. I’m now extracting the observed peak set and checking the masked structure library so that any accepted phase is supported by extinctions, intensities, and the whole pattern—not just the formula.

The dominant peak family is internally consistent with monoclinic wolframite-type MnWO₄: its distinctive 18.68°, 23.89/24.36°, 30.12/30.58°, 36.27/36.62°, and higher-angle families all recur. No precursor pattern explains that set. I’m auditing the atomistic candidate now and will use residuals from a refined one-phase model to test whether any second crystalline phase is actually warranted.

The one-phase model already reaches about 11.7% Rwp with only a single width term, and the refined cell is the expected ambient wolframite cell. A trial with too many global width parameters lowered Rwp but produced negative Gaussian/Lorentzian components and strong correlations, so I’m rolling that branch back. I’ll compare minimal positive-width branches and inspect coherent residual peaks from the valid baseline before deciding on additional phases.

The valid one-phase residual reveals a coherent second family: peaks at about 23.45°, 24.68°, 33.58°, and 34.45°. With the selected specimen displacement, these map directly onto the characteristic split low-angle family of monoclinic WO₃, including its higher-angle companions. I’m carrying the monoclinic, triclinic, and orthorhombic WO₃ alternatives through matched two-phase refinements; overlap at 23.9° alone will not be counted as evidence.

The first valid monoclinic WO₃ model gives a stable positive scale and improves the full-pattern fit from 8.86% to 8.58% Rwp, corresponding provisionally to about 1.9 wt%. Its cell refines only slightly and the phase explains several non-overlapping peaks. The alternative WO₃ symmetry branches are still running under the same nuisance model.

The matched alternatives are separating: orthorhombic WO₃ fits materially worse than monoclinic WO₃ (8.638% vs 8.580% Rwp), while tetragonal WO₃ collapses to essentially zero scale and hexagonal WO₃ gains only 0.013% Rwp. One alternate monoclinic CIF has an unphysical scale/phase-fraction response and is being rejected pending its ADP check. The lower-symmetry triclinic branch is computationally slower and remains under evaluation.

The minor-phase model is now chemically clean and gives 2.03 wt% WO₃ after assigning physically reasonable fixed isotropic displacement parameters; its fraction changes by only about 0.1 wt% from the source-CIF value. I’m simplifying the empirical background because the six-term version leaves a correlation warning even though the profile itself is stable.

The second angular comparison retains the specimen-displacement-only model. A zero-only model is much worse, and the joint zero/displacement model is rejected at 99.7% parameter correlation for a negligible fit change. The mature two-phase profile branches are now testing one additional width term at a time while enforcing positive total widths.

A positive Caglioti U term gives the largest clean width improvement (Rwp 8.58% → 8.18%), and adding a small Lorentzian X term gives a further stable reduction to 8.12%. V is rejected because it is 96.8% correlated with U; the Y branch develops a negative total Lorentzian width. I’m finishing the matched removal/augmentation checks before moving to phase-specific broadening.

The phase inventory is now stable enough to optimize the remaining nuisance and structural terms. I’m continuing from the best two-phase branch (MnWO₄ + monoclinic WO₃), first comparing polarization and surface roughness, then phase-specific broadening and preferred orientation before the final leave-one-out and export audits.

The envelope tests are deliberately matched to the same baseline. The surface-roughness branches are being started from several non-singular parameterizations because the nominal smooth-surface point makes the two Suortti parameters mathematically degenerate; I’ll retain one only if it is positive, stable, and improves the full angular range.

Polarization and one-parameter roughness both reduce the residual strongly, confirming that the dominant remaining mismatch is an intensity envelope rather than a missing bulk phase. The free two-parameter roughness branch is not stabilizing, while its simpler forms are strongly coupled to scale; that correlation will determine whether roughness is defensible despite its lower numerical Rwp.

The four phase-broadening branches are running independently from the polarization model. This is important for the ~2% WO₃ phase: a width parameter will be rejected if it merely absorbs an overlapping MnWO₄ mismatch or runs to an effectively infinite crystallite size, even if the aggregate Rwp changes slightly.

Neither phase supports an independent size or microstrain parameter: MnWO₄ broadening is nearly singular with the global Lorentzian term (and strain turns negative), while WO₃ terms run toward limits or fail to converge for negligible gain. I’m therefore keeping the global positive U/X profile and moving to one-parameter March–Dollase tests on crystallographically distinct axes.

The initial orientation screen shows only a small effect so far (for MnWO₄ [010], Rwp 6.654→6.589% with a ratio near 0.981), and it has not yet reached a low-shift solution. I’m waiting for the other axes before deciding whether that gain is reproducible enough to justify the extra parameter.

Refining only the six symmetry-independent oxygen coordinates gives a larger and chemically meaningful intensity improvement than the texture terms (Rwp 6.654→6.229%), but the first cycle is not converged. I’m continuing that branch and will audit interatomic contacts before accepting it; the high-pressure starting coordinates make this relaxation especially important.

The refined MnWO₄ coordinates pass the contact audit, and a weak but reproducible [100] texture term remains (ratio ≈1.027; Rwp 6.223→6.092%). WO₃ orientation is showing only thousandths of a percent in Rwp and is not likely supportable at its low abundance.

The post-coordinate repeat confirms the same broadening diagnosis: the apparent MnWO₄ strain solution is negative and 94% correlated with X, so it is rejected despite a lower Rwp. The remaining width branches are likewise taking the unstable/limit-seeking route; none will be propagated unless it returns a positive, low-correlation solution.

The mature-model phase retest is now adding audited MnO, Mn₃O₄, and Mn₃WO₆ one at a time with identical nuisance settings. Positive scale alone is not enough: each candidate must survive cell refinement, remain positive, and earn intensity from several of its own reflections rather than overlap the two accepted phases.

The precursor/byproduct retest is decisive: MnO, Mn₃O₄, and Mn₃WO₆ all collapse to the scale floor and then destabilize their cells, so none is retained. I’m now completing the stricter replacement test between the accepted monoclinic WO₃ and its audited triclinic/orthorhombic alternatives under the fully refined MnWO₄ model.

The WO₃ assignment survives the mature leave-one-out test: removing it raises Rwp from 6.092% to 6.383%, with the strongest loss below 30°, while triclinic and orthorhombic replacements both settle near 6.179%. That establishes monoclinic WO₃ as the supported minor structure rather than merely a composition guess.

Matched removals justify U, X, axial divergence, polarization, and the MnWO₄ [100] texture term. W is the exception: fixing it at the calibrated value 5.0 changes Rwp by only 0.00003%, so I’m dropping that refinement variable for a smaller, better-conditioned final model.

The mature roughness retest again lowers Rwp numerically, but it fails the integrity checks: one form sits at the physical A=0 boundary and remains nonconverged, while the non-boundary form is ~93% correlated with scale and depends on an arbitrary fixed A; freeing A previously drove it negative. I’m retaining the interior, reproducible polarization solution rather than reporting an underdetermined roughness correction.

The model now passes the GSAS-II integrity audit with regenerated and saved profiles identical, positive total widths for both phases, no dropped variables, and max shift/esd 0.005. I’m freezing that selected state into `final.gpx`, regenerating its reflections/profile, and exporting all cross-checked deliverables from that exact file.

All required audits pass: `final.gpx` has no numerical/model-integrity flags, both CIFs have no occupancy/geometry/symmetry flags, and the submission readback reports two positive fractions summing exactly to 1. I’m doing one final cross-file numerical comparison so the JSON, CIFs, profile CSV, and GPX cannot silently disagree.

Completed the PXRD analysis and refinement.

- MnWO₄ (P2/c): 98.3065 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃ (P2₁/c): 1.6935 wt%
- Rwp: 6.0921%; Rp: 4.7667%; GOF: 2.2103

The WO₃ fraction has greater systematic uncertainty because of its low abundance and peak overlap. Fractions exclude any unquantified amorphous material.

